4.2. Process to Quantify Driving Workload Using Brainwaves In order to quantify the driving workload by using brainwave signals, a process to handle the signals, as shown in Figure 6, was established. The sampling of the brainwave signals was set at 250 Hz, and data were collected from DAQ equipment. When sampling was set to 250 Hz, a frequency analysis of up to 125 Hz was possible. The collected brainwave signals were classified by road type. Road types come in pairs – straight section with a left turn, straight section with a right turn, and straight section with a curve. For the classified brainwave signals, 250 blocks were established and overlapped by 50%. Then, frequency analysis was performed by calculating the power spectrum using Hanning Window. For 250 blocks, frequency analysis can be carried out once every second by dividing into sampling periods. By using 50% overlap and Hanning, the frequency analysis noise between data blocks can be reduced. By using the average power spectrum size of 8-

